VT_TALK("sql/sql-views", {
 "views": "Some questions get asked again and again, like how much money each customer has spent. A view stores that query under a name, so afterwards you can select from it as if it were a table. Only the query is saved, not the rows, so each time you use the view the engine runs the query again on the current data. Change a product's stock and the low stock view shows the change at once. Teams use views to hide long queries, to show each group only the columns it may see, and to keep one agreed definition of a business rule. In SQLite a view is read only. Think of a saved search in your email app, which shows the latest matching messages each time you open it.",
 "cte": "A common table expression, or CTE, is like a view that lives for just one statement. You write WITH, a name, and a query in brackets, before the main SELECT. The main query, and any later CTE, can read it by that name. This turns a long, nested query into a short list of named steps that you read from top to bottom. In the animation, the first step adds up spending per customer, the second keeps the big spenders, and the last step adds their names. Change the spending limit and watch each step work again. Think of a recipe that says first boil the rice, then fry the onions, then mix them, instead of one long sentence.",
 "compare": "Subqueries, CTEs, views and temporary tables all give a name or a home to an in between result, and they differ in how long they live. A subquery lives in one spot of one query. A CTE lives for one statement. A view lives until you drop it, but stores only the query. A temporary table really stores rows, and disappears when your connection closes, which is useful when several statements reuse the same big result. A materialized view stores the result too, and is refreshed when you ask, which makes slow reports fast at the cost of being a little out of date. Think of the difference between a sticky note, a page in a notebook, and a printed report on the notice board.",
 "levels": "Time for the ladder. For a beginner, a view is a saved question and a CTE is a named step in one big question. At college level, views give data independence, so programs read the view while the tables behind it can change. At masters level, the planner usually folds views and CTEs into the main query as if you had typed the subquery yourself. At research level, people study how to keep materialized views fresh by updating only the rows that changed, called incremental view maintenance. Think of a newspaper that prints a corrections box instead of reprinting the whole paper."
});
